{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction Law good-cause grounds for criminal convictions","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft or drug activity on the premises, or of human trafficking, or who lets such a person reside there. A three-day Notice to Quit must precede suit.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Rental dwellings covered by the Eviction Law, grounds in subsections (o) through (r); suit barred more than two years after conviction, delinquency finding or release from incarceration, whichever is later.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"The drug-activity ground does not apply to a tenant who allows a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Owner-occupied two- and three-family dwelling evictions","requirement":"In a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling a tenant may be removed only by court order on one of six listed causes. Notice to quit is three months for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month, and three days for disorderly or destructive conduct.","key_value":"3 months' notice (at-will/year-to-year); 1 month (month-to-month); 3 days (disorderly, destructive or rule violation)","coverage_conditions":"Landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; causes are staying after lease expiration, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, willful destruction, constant rule violations, or lease violations reserving a right of re-entry.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied dwelling"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for Eviction Law violations","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it for at least six months; one who evicts to board up or demolish must keep the property out of residential use for five years. Violations expose the landlord to treble damages plus fees and costs.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; civil penalty up to $10,000","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who recover possession of a dwelling unit on grounds of personal occupancy, or of permanently boarding up, demolishing or retiring the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000.00 for each violation of the five-year bar.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal against tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, lease alteration, or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, complains in good faith to a government authority, organizes a tenant group, or refuses a lease change made because of those actions.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings exempt"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction notice requirements","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction. Before evicting a tenant because a buyer wants to occupy the foreclosed property, the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days to notify tenants of sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"month-to-month and owner-occupied two- and three-family units are not exempt"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants.","confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Tenants at least 62, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled veterans who lived in the building a year before conversion may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is not more than three times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40 years' protected tenancy; income limit 3 times county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability or veteran criteria and lived there at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The Act extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; at present only Hudson County qualifies.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted; at present the only qualified county is Hudson County.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County only"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"no private kitchens or bathrooms; units occupied by unrelated persons"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibition on rent increases above rent control ordinances","requirement":"A rent increase may not exceed any rent control or rent leveling ordinance; where the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase is prohibited.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental properties subject to a rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where none applies, all rental properties.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":"Local rent control or rent leveling ordinances supply the applicable cap.","confidence":0.4,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
